    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `build_claude` in `app/claude.py`. A test with a stub transport showed all three egress rules working, and `pytest -q` passes (1 test). I didn't add a test for `build_claude` itself.\n\n**Why it doesn't reuse `build_http_client()`:** the pinned `anthropic==1.8.0` runs on `httpx2`, not `httpx`, and it rejects an `httpx.Client` outright. So `build_claude` builds an `httpx2` client with `anthropic.DefaultHttpxClient`. It takes `EGRESS_TAG`, `CONNECT_TIMEOUT`, `READ_TIMEOUT` and the `log_egress` hook from `app/http.py` rather than copying values. `app/http.py` is unchanged, so `fetch_status` works as before.\n\n**What the check showed:** I sent one Claude request through an `httpx2.MockTransport` stub that returned a 500 first and a 200 on the retry:\n- **Header:** both attempts carried `X-Egress-Tag: ops-assistant`.\n- **Logging:** \"app.egress\" logged `POST https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages` exactly once per attempt, retry included.\n- **Timeouts:** connect 5s and read 60s, both on the client and on each request, with `max_retries=2`.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- **The transport has to be an `httpx2` transport** (e.g. `httpx2.MockTransport`). An `httpx` transport like the one in `tests/test_status.py` will be rejected by the SDK. I noted this in the docstring.\n- **Write and pool timeouts are also 60s.** That matches what `app.http.TIMEOUT` does.\n- **If no transport is given,** the SDK's default transport is used, with its connection limits and keepalive settings.\n- **Scratch file to delete:** I couldn't remove `check_claude.py` in the project root because deleting it needed approval. Please delete it."
  },
